  "33": "Tore Ellingsen · Polity, 2024 · Global Challenges course (SSE)",
  "34": "Institutional &amp; Organizational Economics",
  "35": "\u003cspan class=\"en\">A Behavioral Game Theory Introduction.\u003c/span> A comprehensive summary of the preface and chapters 1 to 10 (pp. vii–99, your PDF), followed by chapters 11 to 13 reconstructed from Ellingsen’s course materials: concepts explained, games solved, applications, English vocabulary, flashcards and quizzes.",
  "36": "The book’s question",
  "37": "“How can people be made to help rather than hurt each other?”",
  "38": "How can people be encouraged to help rather than hurt each other? A country, a firm and a couple have one thing in common: they consist of more than one individual. They therefore all face the same \u003cstrong>organizational challenge\u003c/strong> (\u003cspan class=\"en\">organizational challenge\u003c/span>), which existed even before our ancestors became human.",
  "39": "Answer 1",
  "40": "Sacrifice",
  "41": "“Individuals voluntarily make sacrifices for others.”",
  "42": "Based on preferences: altruism (α), a taste for equality (β), a sense of duty (λμ), gratitude (γ), and the desire for esteem. An individual gives even though doing so is costly.",
  "43": "Answer 2",
  "44": "Cooperation",
  "45": "“Individuals coordinate their actions for mutual benefit.”",
  "46": "Always mutual (“it takes two to tango”) and not necessarily sacrificial: selfish people with aligned interests can cooperate perfectly well.",
  "47": "Answer 3",
  "48": "Coercion",
  "49": "“Individuals are forced to take actions that benefit others.”",
  "50": "Credible threats, sanctions, authority, the state (Hobbes’s Leviathan). The next question: who constrains the person who does the constraining?",
  "51": "The thread connecting the chapters",
  "52": "\u003ca href=\"#ch1\">\u003cspan class=\"n\">1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"t\">The Organizational Challenge\u003ci>The organizational challenge\u003c/i>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"k\">The same challenge for countries, firms and couples. Three answers: sacrifice, cooperation, coercion. “Success” depends on the boundaries of the group.\u003c/span>\u003c/a>",
  "53": "\u003ca href=\"#ch2\">\u003cspan class=\"n\">2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"t\">Sacrifice\u003ci>Sacrifice\u003c/i>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"k\">Give $1 so someone else receives $2? Social dilemmas: each person is poorer when giving, but all are richer if all give. Honesty varies enormously across countries.\u003c/span>\u003c/a>",
  "54": "\u003ca href=\"#ch3\">\u003cspan class=\"n\">3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"t\">Selfishness, Rationality, and Utility\u003ci>Selfishness, rationality and utility\u003c/i>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"k\">Selfish ≠ irrational. Expected utility; u = U(c) + λv combines consequences and duty. Evolution, externalities, Pareto efficiency.\u003c/span>\u003c/a>",
  "55": "\u003ca href=\"#ch4\">\u003cspan class=\"n\">4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"t\">Situations, Games, and Cooperation\u003ci>Situations, games and cooperation\u003c/i>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"k\">Situation ≠ game. The Prisoners’ Dilemma, then how α, β and λμ transform it. Self-serving bias. Defining cooperation.\u003c/span>\u003c/a>",
  "56": "\u003ca href=\"#ch5\">\u003cspan class=\"n\">5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"t\">Shared Understandings and Values\u003ci>Shared understandings and values\u003c/i>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"k\">The 5 levels of Fig. 5.1. Culture, institutions (formal/informal, strong/weak). Wallets, the Meiji era, the Scientific Revolution.\u003c/span>\u003c/a>",
  "57": "\u003ca href=\"#ch6\">\u003cspan class=\"n\">6\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"t\">Predicting Behavior in Games\u003ci>Predicting behavior\u003c/i>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"k\">Dominance, Nash equilibrium, mixed strategies, multiple equilibria, sequential games, backward induction, non-credible threats.\u003c/span>\u003c/a>",
  "58": "\u003ca href=\"#ch7\">\u003cspan class=\"n\">7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"t\">A Model of Anarchy\u003ci>A model of anarchy\u003c/i>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"k\">Formalizing Hobbes: without a state, all resources go into weapons. Escaping anarchy: absolute sovereign or social contract?\u003c/span>\u003c/a>",
  "59": "\u003ca href=\"#ch8\">\u003cspan class=\"n\">8\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"t\">Changing the Game\u003ci>Changing the game\u003c/i>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"k\">Communicating, transferring, punishing. Reciprocity, gratitude, grievance, promises and threats (credible if φ is large enough). The hold-up problem.\u003c/span>\u003c/a>",
  "60": "\u003ca href=\"#ch9\">\u003cspan class=\"n\">9\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"t\">Coordination\u003ci>Coordination\u003c/i>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"k\">Common or conflicting interests. Cheap talk (self-committing / self-signaling), Stag Hunt, Chicken, a mediator, common awareness, clarity.\u003c/span>\u003c/a>",
  "61": "\u003ca href=\"#ch10\">\u003cspan class=\"n\">10\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"t\">Authority's Limitations\u003ci>The limits of authority\u003c/i>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"k\">Nominal, real and absolute authority. Too much authority prevents credible promises: the lesson of the Glorious Revolution.\u003c/span>\u003c/a>",
  "62": "\u003ca href=\"#ch11\">\u003cspan class=\"n\">11\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"t\">Relationships\u003ci>Relationships\u003c/i>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"k\">An indefinitely repeated game: the future provides both carrot and stick; cooperation between selfish players becomes an equilibrium if δ is large enough. Retribution and atonement counter renegotiation.\u003c/span>\u003c/a>",
  "63": "\u003ca href=\"#ch12\">\u003cspan class=\"n\">12\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"t\">Third-Party Punishment\u003ci>Third-party punishment\u003c/i>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"k\">A professional enforcer with many clients makes punishment credible: mafias, police, and the absence of a “world police” between states.\u003c/span>\u003c/a>",
  "64": "\u003ca href=\"#ch13\">\u003cspan class=\"n\">13\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"t\">Coercion: Costs and Benefits\u003ci>Costs and benefits of coercion\u003c/i>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"k\">Coercion solves the multilateral free-rider problem, but enables extortion and undermines the authorities’ promises.\u003c/span>\u003c/a>",
  "65": "The book’s conceptual map \u003cspan class=\"tag\">Fig. 5.1 · p. 37\u003c/span>",
  "66": "This diagram captures the book’s entire logic. It begins with reality, which every society \u003cstrong>understands\u003c/strong> and \u003cstrong>evaluates\u003c/strong> in its own way. These understandings and values give rise to \u003cstrong>norms\u003c/strong>. Norms shape what each person \u003cstrong>expects\u003c/strong> of others and what they \u003cstrong>prefer\u003c/strong>, and these two elements determine \u003cstrong>actions\u003c/strong>. Chapters 3, 4 and 6 work at the bottom of the diagram (preferences, expectations, actions). Chapter 5 moves up through its levels, and chapters 8 to 10 ask how to change them.",
  "67": "The five levels: reality; understandings and values; prescriptive norms; expectations and preferences; actions.",
  "68": "indiv. &amp; social",
  "69": "social",
  "70": "individual",
  "71": "individual",
  "72": "Reality",
  "73": "reality",
